DDA algorithm to traverse grid cells along ray:

Transform to grid coordinates:
`u = (x + radius) / cell`, `v = (radius - y) / cell`.
Indices: `i = Math.floor(u)`, `j = Math.floor(v)`.
Direction in grid: `du = dx / cell`, `dv = -dy / cell` (since v increases as y 
decreases). Actually if we use d in metres, u(d) = (x + dx*d + radius)/cell, so 
du per metre = dx/cell. v(d) = (radius - (y+dy*d))/cell = (radius-y)/cell - 
dy*d/cell, so dv = -dy/cell.

We want step through cells crossed by ray for d>0. Standard voxel traversal:
- tMaxX = distance (in d, metres) to next vertical grid line, tMaxY to next 
horizontal line.
- tDeltaX = cell/|dx|, tDeltaY = cell/|dy|.
- iStep = sign(dx), jStep = sign(dy).
- Start at current cell i,j. We can skip it (start after d=0) or check it. Then 
loop while cell inside grid:
  - if tMaxX < tMaxY: i += iStep; d = tMaxX; tMaxX += tDeltaX; else j += jStep; 
d = tMaxY; tMaxY += tDeltaY.
  - if i<0||... break (venue ends)
  - check cell (i + j*g) top > z + d*tanAlt.
- Need handle dx=0 or dy=0: tMax infinite.

This exactly traverses all cells the ray passes through. But if ray starts 
exactly on boundary, potential issues; not critical. Also if ray goes out of 
grid, break. This is efficient and avoids missing.

